+- **Loan options.** When the money in hand falls short of the investment, `/advisory` shows one card for each loan length (12, 24, 36 and 60 months). A card is the largest loan, in whole thousands of rupees and no bigger than the shortfall, whose repayment still counts as low pressure (under 30% of the monthly surplus) and keeps the household check within half of its income, at the same assumed 12% a year the risk figure uses. The rules mark the shortest length that covers the whole shortfall, and a card that does not cover it says how much is still missing. When no loan is safe, the page says why (no surplus, a household already over its limit, or no room left after the current loans). These are planning figures and not a quote: no lender has offered these terms, and nothing on the page says one will. Each card's total is the sum of its schedule, to the paisa; `/report` prints that schedule, one row a month (the payment, the interest, the part that repays the loan, what is still owed), with a menu to pick the length. The last payment differs a little from the rest, because it clears what the whole-rupee payments leave over. Beside it one line says how many times the money left each month covers the monthly loan payments, the loans already being paid included. Banks call that DSCR and may work it out differently. It is the repayment-pressure figure read the other way up, so it adds no new test of the plan, and no lender's cut-off is attached to it.
+- **What the schemes put in and pay.** For PMEGP, PMFME and Mudra, when the matcher finds them, `/advisory` and `/report` show a card of what the reader would put in, what the scheme adds and what is left to borrow, worked out on the money the reader said they need to start. PMEGP is shown twice, once for the general category and once for a special one (women, SC, ST, OBC, minorities and others its guidelines name), because the app never asks which the reader is and the card leaves them to read their own. PMEGP counts at most ₹20 lakh of the cost, and PMFME's government money stops at ₹10 lakh a unit. Mudra has no subsidy and no stated own share, so the card names the loan size the money still needed falls in. These are sums on the terms each scheme has published, with a caveat for each (see the limits at the end). The card checks no eligibility, does not test whether the repayment fits the household, and says its figures are estimates and not an offer. Python only, no model.
+- **Registrations to check.** `/advisory` and `/report` list four things to ask about for the reader's kind of work: Udyam registration (free, done online), the local office (the Gram Panchayat in a village, the municipal or town office elsewhere), FSSAI for a food business, and GST. Each step is marked "Usually needed", "Depends on your business", "Probably not needed", "Free to register" or "Ask the office", and names the published source it comes from with the date it was read: the FSSAI licensing rules (as compiled on 9 November 2017), the CBIC booklet on GST (1 June 2019) and the Udyam portal (28 October 2026). The limits are tested against the reader's expected monthly income times 12, and the card says so. It decides nothing, it never says a registration is required, and it ends by telling the reader to confirm with the office concerned. The limits are constants in `compliance_service.py`; which kinds of work count as food, goods or services is our reading, and the card does not hide that.
+- **The written plan.** With the helper on, `/advisory` ends in a card that can write a short plan in the reader's language. The card has its own unticked box, because pressing the button sends a description of the business to Meta; the box is cleared after every plan. The plan is the model's wording with the rules' figures put in, in bold, and it is labelled as AI-written. It takes about ten seconds. If the model fails, the checks refuse its reply twice, or the spend cap is reached, the card says so and the rest of the page is unchanged.
